# Interface members are absent from the symbol table: no I::method symbols, dead methods field

## Steps to Reproduce

1. TODO: Add steps

## Expected Behavior

TODO: Describe expected behavior

## Actual Behavior

TODO: Describe actual behavior

Found by the B049 lane, 2026-08-30, and verified in the code rather than
inferred. Pre-existing; independent of B049, which only had to work around it.

## Defect
`symbols/table.rs:437` is the ONLY `Item::Interface` arm. It pushes a single
symbol with `methods: Vec::new()` **hardcoded** and never descends into
`iface.methods`. Compare the `Item::Class` arm immediately around it, which
does descend and emits `C::member` symbols.

Consequences:
- No `I::method` symbol ever enters the pool.
- The `methods` field on the interface symbol is **dead**: every consumer in
  the codebase matches `Interface { .. }` and ignores it, and the only
  construction with content is a test passing `vec![]`.

## Evidence
For:

    interface I { void Go(); }
    class C : I { void Go() {} }
    void f(I@ i) { i.Go(); }

the symbol pool contains `I`, `C`, `C::Go`, `f` — and **no `I::Go`**.
`goto_definition` on `i.Go()` returns **None**.

So Go to Definition is already broken on interface members today, with no
connection to rename. Hover, completion and workspace-symbols read the same
pool, so they are all affected to whatever degree they depend on it.

## How B049 worked around it
B049's rename guard reads `iface.methods` from the ASTs directly. That is
appropriate for a rename guard — it must not rewrite an interface declaration
when renaming a same-spelled free function — but it is the WRONG general fix:
the symbols should exist in the table, and every other consumer still cannot
see them. Do not copy the workaround; remove the need for it.

## Work
- Make the `Item::Interface` arm descend into `iface.methods` and emit
  `I::method` symbols, mirroring the `Item::Class` arm.
- Then either populate the `methods` field or delete it. A field that is
  always empty and read by nobody is worse than no field — it reads as
  supported.
- Re-check B049's rename guard afterwards: once real `I::method` symbols
  exist, the AST-reading workaround may be replaceable by the normal path.
  Do not remove it without a red test proving the replacement holds.

## Test plan (TDD, red first)
Red: `goto_definition` on `i.Go()` in the snippet above returns None today
and must resolve to the interface declaration after.

Variants (per CLAUDE.md, generalize):
- an interface method also implemented by a class -> both are findable and
  distinguishable
- multiple interfaces declaring the same method name
- an interface method never implemented
- interface inheritance, if the language allows it
- hover and completion on an interface-typed receiver
- workspace-symbols lists `I::method`
- renaming a free function that shares a spelling with an interface method
  still leaves the interface untouched (B049's guarantee must survive)

## Gate
FP fixture ERROR counts must not RISE — adding symbols can change resolution
outcomes, so measure two binaries on the same machine at the same time
(baselines 14/1485, 5/276, 2/35, 4/2, 4/4, showcase 9/7). A drop is plausible
if any fixture calls an interface method; explain it rather than accepting it.

